Chapter: IdtInspector
  cites Dev chapter HexFormat
  grounds Device.Port

 The interrupt half of the developer debugger: the dispatch table the
 processor will actually use, the two counters the ISRs actually move, and
 what the machine actually recorded the last time it stopped advancing.

 THE TABLE IS AT A COMPILE-TIME CONSTANT AND IS NOT READ FROM IDTR. The
 boot path builds 256 gates at `idt-base` and then points IDTR at that
 same address, so the constant and the register agree by construction and
 an `sidt` would answer what is written here. That is worth stating rather
 than assuming, because it is the one thing this chapter cannot check: if
 something ever loaded a SECOND table, every line below would describe the
 table that is no longer installed. Reading IDTR needs a builtin that does
 not exist, and inventing one for this was more compiler work than the
 reading is worth.

 THE ENTRY LAYOUT IS READ OFF THE EMITTER, not off a manual. `emit-idt-
 entries` writes each gate as two stores: a qword at +0 carrying the low
 offset word and selector 8, then a qword at +4 carrying `0x8E00` in its
 low half, which is what puts the type in the nibble at +5 and the present
 bit above it. Every vector therefore reads back type 14, DPL 0 and
 present, and the stubs are twelve bytes apart, so vector N's handler is
 vector zero's plus twelve N. Vector 8 is the single exception: the boot
 path writes 1 to the IST field at +4 afterwards so a double fault lands
 on the emergency stack. An inspector that showed all 256 gates identical
 would be hiding the one gate whose difference is load-bearing.

 THERE IS NO EXCEPTION RECORD ON THIS MACHINE, and `ii-fault-report` is
 what exists instead. Nothing in the address map stores a faulting vector,
 RIP or RSP; the only cells that ever hold a RIP are the watchdog's, and
 the timer ISR writes them when neither the heap pointer nor the saved RIP
 has moved since the last tick. So a slot is a sample of a system that had
 stopped advancing, which is close to the question an operator asks after
 a hang and is NOT the same as a trap frame. Naming it "the last fault"
 would be inventing a record the machine does not keep.

 THE RING IS FOUR SLOTS AND THE HEAD IS A FREE-RUNNING COUNTER. The
 writer masks with three and scales by thirty-two, so the head is a total
 count of samples and not an index; the newest slot is the one before it.
 Reporting them oldest-first would put the sample that matters last.

 EVERYTHING HERE EXCEPT THE PIC READ IS `peek`, WHICH COSTS NOTHING. That
 is why these reports are built where they are asked for rather than
 cached, and it is the opposite of the disk inspector next door, where
 every read is 512 bytes of heap that is never returned.

 We say:

Section: The Table

  ii-idt-base : Integer = 24576
  ii-vector-count : Integer = 256
  ii-entry-size : Integer = 16

  ii-entry-addr : Integer -> Integer
  ii-entry-addr (vec) = ii-idt-base + vec * ii-entry-size

  ii-u16-at : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ii-u16-at (a) (off) = bit-or (peek-byte a off) (bit-shl (peek-byte a (off + 1)) 8)

Section: One Gate

 The offset is assembled from three fields the processor keeps apart, and
 it is the field an operator is actually after: it is the address the
 machine will jump to, and it is what a symbol lookup takes.

  ii-gate-offset : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-offset (a) =
    let lo = ii-u16-at a 0
    in let mid = bit-shl (ii-u16-at a 6) 16
    in let hi = bit-shl (peek-32 a 8) 32
    in bit-or lo (bit-or mid hi)

  ii-gate-selector : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-selector (a) = ii-u16-at a 2

  ii-gate-ist : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-ist (a) = bit-and (peek-byte a 4) 7

  ii-gate-attr : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-attr (a) = peek-byte a 5

  ii-gate-type : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-type (a) = bit-and (ii-gate-attr a) 15

  ii-gate-dpl : Integer -> Integer
  ii-gate-dpl (a) = bit-and (bit-shru (ii-gate-attr a) 5) 3

  ii-gate-present : Integer -> Boolean
  ii-gate-present (a) = bit-and (ii-gate-attr a) 128 /= 0

 THE TYPE IS NAMED ONLY WHERE THE NAME IS CERTAIN. An unknown type is
 reported as its number rather than guessed at, for the same reason the
 fact log reports a raw kind: an inspector that renames the thing it is
 inspecting is worth less than no inspector.

  ii-gate-type-name : Integer -> Text
  ii-gate-type-name (t) =
    if t == 14 then "interrupt"
    else if t == 15 then "trap"
    else if t == 5 then "task"
    else "type " & show t

  ii-present-word : Integer -> Text
  ii-present-word (a) = if ii-gate-present a then "present" else "NOT PRESENT"

  ii-gate-attrs : Integer -> Text
  ii-gate-attrs (a) =
    let t = ii-gate-type-name (ii-gate-type a)
    in t & " dpl " & show (ii-gate-dpl a) & " ist " & show (ii-gate-ist a) & " " & ii-present-word a

  ii-gate-line : Integer -> Text
  ii-gate-line (vec) =
    let a = ii-entry-addr vec
    in let head = "vec " & show vec & "  off " & addr-to-hex (ii-gate-offset a)
    in head & "  sel #" & u16-to-hex (ii-gate-selector a) & "  " & ii-gate-attrs a

 A vector outside the table is REFUSED and names the value it rejected.
 `ii-entry-addr` of 300 is an address inside whatever follows the table,
 and it would decode into a confident, entirely fictional gate.

  ii-entry-report : Integer -> List Text
  ii-entry-report (vec) =
    if vec < 0 then [ii-vector-refusal vec]
    else if vec >= ii-vector-count then [ii-vector-refusal vec]
    else [ii-gate-line vec]

  ii-vector-refusal : Integer -> Text
  ii-vector-refusal (vec) =
    "refused: vector " & show vec & " is not in 0.." & show (ii-vector-count - 1)

Section: The Counters

 Both cells are zeroed before `sti` and moved only by an ISR, so zero
 means the handler has never run rather than that the count wrapped.
 Naming the two states apart is the whole value of the command: a tick
 count of zero on a running system says the timer never armed, and that
 reads identically to a slow boot if the line just prints a number.

  ii-tick-count-addr : Integer = 28672
  ii-key-buffer-addr : Integer = 28680

  ii-tick-line : Text
  ii-tick-line =
    let ticks = peek-qword ii-tick-count-addr 0
    in if ticks == 0 then "ticks 0 -- the timer ISR has not run"
    else "ticks " & show ticks

 The keyboard cell holds the last scancode the ISR read from port 0x60,
 zero-extended, and NOTHING CLEARS IT. The read syscall loads it without
 zeroing, so this is the last key seen and not a queue depth; a stale
 non-zero value is the normal resting state after any keypress.

  ii-key-line : Text
  ii-key-line =
    let k = bit-and (peek-qword ii-key-buffer-addr 0) 255
    in if k == 0 then "last scancode none -- the keyboard ISR has not run"
    else "last scancode #" & hexfmt-byte-to-hex k & " (" & show k & ")"

  ii-counts-report : List Text
  ii-counts-report = [ii-tick-line, ii-key-line]

Section: The Stall Ring

  ii-wd-stale-tick-addr : Integer = 28744
  ii-wd-ring-buf-addr : Integer = 28768
  ii-wd-ring-head-addr : Integer = 28896
  ii-wd-slots : Integer = 4
  ii-wd-slot-size : Integer = 32

  ii-fault-header : Integer -> Text
  ii-fault-header (head) =
    let stale = peek-qword ii-wd-stale-tick-addr 0
    in show head & " stall samples taken, stale-tick counter " & show stale

 Newest first. The slot is the head minus one minus the row, and the
 whole table is offset by a multiple of the slot count before the modulo
 so the arithmetic never reaches a negative index.

  ii-fault-slot : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ii-fault-slot (head) (i) =
    int-mod (head - 1 - i + ii-wd-slots * ii-wd-slots) ii-wd-slots

  ii-fault-body : Integer, Integer -> Text
  ii-fault-body (i) (a) =
    let head = "-" & show i & "  rip " & addr-to-hex (peek-qword a 0)
    in head & "  rsp " & addr-to-hex (peek-qword a 8) & "  heap " & addr-to-hex (peek-qword a 16)

  ii-fault-line : Integer, Integer -> Text
  ii-fault-line (head) (i) =
    ii-fault-body i (ii-wd-ring-buf-addr + ii-fault-slot head i * ii-wd-slot-size)

 A ring with fewer samples than slots must not print the slots it has not
 written yet: `alloc-bytes` does not zero, and neither did anything else
 pass over this band, so an unwritten slot holds whatever the firmware
 left and would render as a plausible RIP.

  ii-fault-lines : Integer, Integer -> List Text
  ii-fault-lines (head) (i) =
    if i >= ii-wd-slots then []
    else if i >= head then []
    else [ii-fault-line head i] & ii-fault-lines head (i + 1)

  ii-fault-report : List Text
  ii-fault-report =
    let head = peek-qword ii-wd-ring-head-addr 0
    in if head == 0 then ["no stall sample since boot -- the watchdog has not fired"]
    else [ii-fault-header head] & ii-fault-lines head 0

 The newest slot on its own, for the console views that want a RIP and an
 RSP rather than a table. `ii-stall-sampled` GUARDS BOTH READERS and is
 not decoration: with an empty ring the newest-slot arithmetic lands on
 slot three, which has never been written, so an unguarded read answers
 whatever the firmware left there. That is precisely the failure these
 two functions were written to repair, and repeating it one address to
 the left would not be an improvement.

  ii-stall-sampled : Boolean
  ii-stall-sampled = peek-qword ii-wd-ring-head-addr 0 /= 0

  ii-stall-newest-addr : Integer
  ii-stall-newest-addr =
    ii-wd-ring-buf-addr + ii-fault-slot (peek-qword ii-wd-ring-head-addr 0) 0 * ii-wd-slot-size

  ii-stall-rip : Integer
  ii-stall-rip = if ii-stall-sampled then peek-qword ii-stall-newest-addr 0 else 0

  ii-stall-rsp : Integer
  ii-stall-rsp = if ii-stall-sampled then peek-qword ii-stall-newest-addr 8 else 0

Section: The Interrupt Controller

 Three registers and not two. The design names ISR and IRR, which answer
 what is in service and what is pending; neither answers why a line that
 should be firing is silent, and that is the mask. It is one more port
 read on a register that needs no OCW3 at all.

  ii-pic1-cmd : Integer = 32
  ii-pic1-data : Integer = 33
  ii-pic2-cmd : Integer = 160
  ii-pic2-data : Integer = 161
  ii-ocw3-irr : Integer = 10
  ii-ocw3-isr : Integer = 11

 An OCW3 write LATCHES which register the next read of the command port
 returns, so the write and the read are a pair and cannot be separated or
 reordered. Reading both PICs means four writes and four reads, in that
 interleaving.

  ii-pic-read : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ii-pic-read (cmd-port) (ocw3) =
    let w = port-out-byte cmd-port ocw3
    in port-in-byte cmd-port

  ii-bits-line : Text, Integer -> Text
  ii-bits-line (label) (v) =
    let head = label & " #" & hexfmt-byte-to-hex v & " "
    in head & ii-bit-names v 0 ""

 The lines are named by bit rather than printed as a byte alone. An
 operator reading `#04` has to count to know it is IRQ 2, and IRQ 2 on the
 master is the cascade, which is the one line whose meaning is not its
 number.

  ii-bit-names : Integer, Integer, Text -> Text
  ii-bit-names (v) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= 8 then ii-bits-or-none acc
    else if bit-and v (bit-shl 1 i) /= 0 then ii-bit-names v (i + 1) (ii-bit-join acc i)
    else ii-bit-names v (i + 1) acc

  ii-bit-join : Text, Integer -> Text
  ii-bit-join (acc) (i) =
    if text-length acc == 0 then show i else acc & " " & show i

  ii-bits-or-none : Text -> Text
  ii-bits-or-none (acc) = if text-length acc == 0 then "(none)" else acc

  ii-irq-report : [Device.Port] List Text
  ii-irq-report =
    let m-irr = ii-pic-read ii-pic1-cmd ii-ocw3-irr
    in let m-isr = ii-pic-read ii-pic1-cmd ii-ocw3-isr
    in let m-imr = port-in-byte ii-pic1-data
    in let s-irr = ii-pic-read ii-pic2-cmd ii-ocw3-irr
    in let s-isr = ii-pic-read ii-pic2-cmd ii-ocw3-isr
    in let s-imr = port-in-byte ii-pic2-data
    in [ii-bits-line "master irr" m-irr, ii-bits-line "master isr" m-isr,
        ii-bits-line "master imr" m-imr, ii-bits-line "slave  irr" s-irr,
        ii-bits-line "slave  isr" s-isr, ii-bits-line "slave  imr" s-imr]
